1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,128
A shopkeeper adds kilograms to kilograms.

2
00:00:03,128 --> 00:00:05,353
A clock adds minutes to minutes.

3
00:00:05,753 --> 00:00:08,244
But nobody adds kilograms to minutes.

4
00:00:08,244 --> 00:00:09,144
Why not?

5
00:00:09,024 --> 00:00:11,789
That one rule can catch a wrong formula.

6
00:00:12,239 --> 00:00:17,302
Aarav's classmate writes: distance equals
speed times time, plus acceleration

7
00:00:17,302 --> 00:00:18,304
times time.

8
00:00:18,654 --> 00:00:20,125
Is it even possible?

9
00:00:20,125 --> 00:00:23,520
Aarav wants to know before
doing a single experiment.

10
00:00:24,020 --> 00:00:25,810
Today: a ten-second test.

11
00:00:26,410 --> 00:00:27,764
Quick reminder.

12
00:00:27,764 --> 00:00:29,010
Length is L.

13
00:00:29,010 --> 00:00:30,134
Time is T.

14
00:00:30,134 --> 00:00:31,259
Mass is M.

15
00:00:31,659 --> 00:00:33,929
Speed is LT to the minus one.

16
00:00:34,329 --> 00:00:37,185
Acceleration is LT to the minus two.

17
00:00:37,835 --> 00:00:39,185
Now the big rule.

18
00:00:39,185 --> 00:00:42,802
It is called the principle
of homogeneity of dimensions.

19
00:00:43,152 --> 00:00:44,097
Here it is.

20
00:00:44,097 --> 00:00:48,156
In a correct equation, every term
must have the same dimensions.

21
00:00:48,856 --> 00:00:52,804
You can only add, subtract or
equate things of the same kind.

22
00:00:53,204 --> 00:00:56,125
Three metres plus two
metres is five metres.

23
00:00:56,125 --> 00:00:59,566
But three metres plus two
seconds is nothing at all.

24
00:01:00,216 --> 00:01:02,444
Let's test this on Aarav's bicycle.

25
00:01:02,444 --> 00:01:05,698
It starts at two metres
per second and speeds up.

26
00:01:06,048 --> 00:01:06,948
Watch.

27
00:01:06,723 --> 00:01:09,224
Speed times time grows in metres.

28
00:01:09,224 --> 00:01:12,135
Half a t squared also grows in metres.

29
00:01:12,535 --> 00:01:16,309
Add them, and you get x, the
distance gone, also in metres.

30
00:01:16,309 --> 00:01:17,820
The graph curves upward.

31
00:01:18,220 --> 00:01:19,350
Slow-mo replay.

32
00:01:19,350 --> 00:01:21,885
Velocity, in blue, keeps growing.

33
00:01:21,885 --> 00:01:24,695
Acceleration, in red, stays the same.

34
00:01:25,094 --> 00:01:27,257
Now his last term, a times t.

35
00:01:27,257 --> 00:01:29,565
It comes out in metres per second.

36
00:01:29,965 --> 00:01:31,910
Metres plus metres per second?

37
00:01:31,910 --> 00:01:34,491
That is like adding kilograms to minutes.

38
00:01:34,491 --> 00:01:35,810
It cannot be done.

39
00:01:36,460 --> 00:01:40,121
Let's check the correct formula
properly, one step at a time.

40
00:01:40,471 --> 00:01:41,371
Step one.

41
00:01:41,277 --> 00:01:43,605
The left side, x, is a length.

42
00:01:43,605 --> 00:01:45,119
Its dimensions are L.

43
00:01:45,869 --> 00:01:46,769
Step two.

44
00:01:46,737 --> 00:01:48,811
u t is velocity times time.

45
00:01:48,811 --> 00:01:51,986
LT to the minus one, times T, gives L.

46
00:01:52,736 --> 00:01:53,636
Step three.

47
00:01:53,635 --> 00:01:55,500
The half is a pure number.

48
00:01:55,500 --> 00:01:58,879
LT to the minus two,
times T squared, gives L.

49
00:01:59,629 --> 00:02:00,570
Step four.

50
00:02:00,570 --> 00:02:01,965
Every term is L.

51
00:02:01,965 --> 00:02:05,003
So the equation is
dimensionally consistent.

52
00:02:05,703 --> 00:02:07,664
Now the classmate's formula.

53
00:02:07,664 --> 00:02:10,562
Its last term is acceleration times time.

54
00:02:10,962 --> 00:02:13,077
That is LT to the minus one.

55
00:02:13,077 --> 00:02:14,774
A speed, not a length.

56
00:02:14,774 --> 00:02:16,870
So the formula is surely wrong.

57
00:02:17,520 --> 00:02:19,512
But why must every term match?

58
00:02:19,512 --> 00:02:20,720
Follow the links.

59
00:02:21,070 --> 00:02:23,571
A true law must stay true in any unit.

60
00:02:23,571 --> 00:02:25,914
Let's run the bicycle for two seconds.

61
00:02:26,314 --> 00:02:28,784
In seconds, both formulas give six.

62
00:02:28,784 --> 00:02:30,792
His formula even looks right!

63
00:02:31,192 --> 00:02:33,559
Now measure the same time in minutes.

64
00:02:33,559 --> 00:02:36,209
The correct formula
still gives six metres.

65
00:02:36,609 --> 00:02:39,947
But his last term jumps
to one hundred and twenty.

66
00:02:39,947 --> 00:02:43,176
Now his formula says one
hundred and twenty four.

67
00:02:43,576 --> 00:02:45,210
Same ride, two answers.

68
00:02:45,210 --> 00:02:49,914
A mixed formula changes with the unit,
so it can never be a law of nature.

69
00:02:50,564 --> 00:02:51,636
Side by side.

70
00:02:51,636 --> 00:02:53,929
With a half, the formula passes.

71
00:02:53,929 --> 00:02:57,045
It gives six metres,
just like the real ride.

72
00:02:57,445 --> 00:03:01,241
With a two, it still passes,
since a number has no dimensions.

73
00:03:01,241 --> 00:03:02,886
But it says twelve metres.

74
00:03:02,886 --> 00:03:03,786
Wrong.

75
00:03:04,028 --> 00:03:05,036
Now you try.

76
00:03:05,036 --> 00:03:09,060
If a formula passes the dimension
check, is it surely correct?

77
00:03:09,060 --> 00:03:10,920
Pause the video and decide.

78
00:03:14,120 --> 00:03:15,020
No!

79
00:03:14,600 --> 00:03:16,415
Fails means surely wrong.

80
00:03:16,415 --> 00:03:18,710
Passes means only possibly right.

81
00:03:18,710 --> 00:03:21,068
Numbers like a half slip through.

82
00:03:21,718 --> 00:03:22,869
One more rule.

83
00:03:22,869 --> 00:03:27,659
Whatever goes inside sine, cosine,
log or e to the power must be a pure

84
00:03:27,659 --> 00:03:28,559
number.

85
00:03:28,731 --> 00:03:31,808
An angle is arc length divided by radius.

86
00:03:31,808 --> 00:03:33,335
Length over length.

87
00:03:33,335 --> 00:03:35,670
So an angle has no dimensions.

88
00:03:36,270 --> 00:03:37,233
Your turn.

89
00:03:37,233 --> 00:03:40,389
In y equals A sine omega t, t is time.

90
00:03:40,389 --> 00:03:43,180
What must the dimensions of omega be?

91
00:03:43,180 --> 00:03:44,597
Pause and decide.

92
00:03:47,797 --> 00:03:49,381
T to the minus one!

93
00:03:49,381 --> 00:03:54,184
Omega times t must be a pure number,
so omega has to cancel the T.

94
00:03:54,834 --> 00:03:56,570
Now, one worked example.

95
00:03:56,920 --> 00:04:00,970
Check the formula v squared
equals u squared plus two a s.

96
00:04:00,970 --> 00:04:02,048
Then use it.

97
00:04:02,548 --> 00:04:07,490
v squared and u squared both have
dimensions L squared T to the minus two.

98
00:04:08,240 --> 00:04:11,477
Two a s is LT to the minus two, times L.

99
00:04:11,477 --> 00:04:13,997
Also L squared T to the minus two.

100
00:04:13,997 --> 00:04:14,900
It passes.

101
00:04:15,650 --> 00:04:20,319
Now a cycle starts at three metres per
second, and speeds up at two metres

102
00:04:20,319 --> 00:04:22,835
per second squared, over four metres.

103
00:04:23,585 --> 00:04:27,243
v squared is nine plus
sixteen, which is twenty five.

104
00:04:27,243 --> 00:04:30,333
So the final speed is
five metres per second.

105
00:04:30,983 --> 00:04:32,445
Now, the exam trap.

106
00:04:32,795 --> 00:04:37,808
Many students think every quantity in an
equation must have the same dimensions.

107
00:04:38,208 --> 00:04:39,108
Not true.

108
00:04:39,095 --> 00:04:42,548
Only terms that are added
or equated must match.

109
00:04:42,548 --> 00:04:47,343
In F equals m a, mass and
acceleration differ, and that is fine.

110
00:04:47,993 --> 00:04:49,392
Where is this asked?

111
00:04:49,392 --> 00:04:53,552
As questions on the dimensions of
constants, or on the formula that is

112
00:04:53,552 --> 00:04:54,452
wrong.

113
00:04:54,725 --> 00:04:55,973
Exam level now.

114
00:04:55,973 --> 00:04:59,568
x equals alpha sine beta
t, plus gamma t squared.

115
00:04:59,568 --> 00:05:02,994
What are the dimensions
of alpha beta over gamma?

116
00:05:02,994 --> 00:05:04,295
Pause and solve.

117
00:05:09,695 --> 00:05:10,996
The answer is C.

118
00:05:10,996 --> 00:05:12,650
The dimensions of time.

119
00:05:13,150 --> 00:05:14,488
Alpha is a length.

120
00:05:14,488 --> 00:05:18,577
Beta t must be a pure number,
so beta is T to the minus one.

121
00:05:19,077 --> 00:05:23,554
Gamma t squared is a length, so
gamma is LT to the minus two.

122
00:05:23,554 --> 00:05:25,674
Divide, and only T is left.

123
00:05:26,274 --> 00:05:27,736
Here is your recap.

124
00:05:27,736 --> 00:05:29,239
Screenshot this card.

125
00:05:32,439 --> 00:05:35,276
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

126
00:05:35,676 --> 00:05:36,576
One.

127
00:05:36,247 --> 00:05:39,386
Why can distance never be u t plus a t?

128
00:05:40,286 --> 00:05:41,186
Two.

129
00:05:40,794 --> 00:05:44,886
If a formula passes the dimension
check, is it surely correct?

130
00:05:45,786 --> 00:05:46,686
Three.

131
00:05:46,476 --> 00:05:51,207
In y equals A sine omega t, what
are the dimensions of omega?

132
00:05:52,307 --> 00:05:56,127
Next time: can this rule
build a formula from nothing?

133
00:05:56,127 --> 00:05:58,575
Applications of dimensional analysis.
